      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Nascent tumors are mostly eliminated by the immune system. During carcinogenesis mutated cells find a way to escape from the immune system´s surveillance. As the tumor microenvironment evolves it becomes increasingly difficult for T cells to recognize and kill cancer cells. Recently, novel immunological targets have been recognized and potent immunomodulatory drugs discovered in clinical trials. This has resulted in the emergence of immunotherapy as a novel potent therapy for cancer in addition to chemotherapy, targeted therapy, operative therapy and radiotherapy."},
